var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-faunus"]=(()=>{var t=Object.defineProperty;var r=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(a,e)=>{for(var n in e)t(a,n,{get:e[n],enumerable:!0})},u=(a,e,n,i)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let o of h(e))!d.call(a,o)&&o!==n&&t(a,o,{get:()=>e[o],enumerable:!(i=r(e,o))||i.enumerable});return a};var c=a=>u(t({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),a);var m={};l(m,{journey:()=>s});var s={id:"deity-faunus",kind:"deity",paths:["roman"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/faunus",title:"Faunus of the Woods",summary:"A month with Faunus, the Roman god of woods, pasture and flocks: the old king of Latium who became a god, the voices heard in the forest, Horace\u2019s country feast on the Nones of December, the Lupercalia told honestly, and a practice of listening outdoors and caring for animals.",welcome:`This journey runs about a lunar month and happens mostly outdoors, in whatever green place you can reach: a wood, a field edge, a park, a garden, even a tree on your street. You will learn who Faunus was to the Romans, how he spoke in the woods, how country people feasted him, what the Lupercalia was and was not, and how people honour him today. There is a nine-day practice of listening and care, and at the end you may make a self-dedication.

It is a self-dedication, not an initiation. The Lupercalia had its own brotherhood, the Luperci, and nothing here revives it. What anyone can do is what Horace\u2019s country neighbours did: give Faunus milk, wine and incense, ask him to keep the flocks and fields, and look after the animals and wild places in their care.

Faunus is a little wild. Go gently, keep your sense of humour, and stay safe outdoors.`,about:"Faunus is the Roman god of the woods and pastures, of flocks and herds and the people who keep them. Roman writers made him an early king of Latium, son of Picus and grandson of Saturn, and father of King Latinus, who was honoured as a god after his death. He spoke: his name was explained from fari, \u201Cto speak\u201D, and his voice was heard in lonely woods, in the rustle of leaves and in dreams at his oracle in the grove of Albunea. There was also a whole kind of beings called fauni, playful spirits of the countryside. Romans matched him with the Greek Pan, and pictured him the same way, horned and goat-legged. He had a temple on Tiber Island, dedicated on the Ides of February, and country people kept his feast on the Nones of December with dancing, wine and incense. Ovid ties him to the Lupercalia on 15 February, though which god that strange festival honoured was argued even in antiquity. Today Roman polytheists honour him at the Faunalia and in February, and many modern Pagans love him as a god of wild places, often blending him with Pan.",notes:["The ancient Lupercalia sacrificed goats and a dog, and young men ran almost naked striking passers-by with strips of goatskin. It is described here as history and is not to be recreated in any form.","Faunus was matched with the Greek Pan, and Pan in turn shaped the modern Horned God of Wicca. These are related but different figures. The journey keeps the Roman god in front and says where the others come in.","Some of the stories about Faunus and the fauni are about chasing nymphs, and Ovid tells one comic tale of an attempted assault. The journey says so plainly.","Stay safe outdoors: tell someone where you are going, keep to paths you know, take care with livestock and wild animals, and leave no food that could harm animals. Never light fires in woods or fields.","Modern Roman polytheism is small and varied. The gods of Rome belong to anyone who approaches them with respect, whatever their ancestry."],holyDays:["lupercalia"],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"Listening at the edge of the wood",intro:"Before study, a greeting, outdoors if you can. Faunus is a god who speaks in quiet places, so the first rite is mostly listening. It follows the Roman form, with the head covered, Janus first and Vesta last, made simple enough to carry in a pocket.",days:2,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"faunus-threshold-listen",title:"Five minutes of listening",instructions:"On each of the two days, go to the greenest place you can easily reach and stand or sit still for five minutes with your eyes half closed. Listen: birds, wind in leaves, insects, animals, distant voices. Romans said the voices of Faunus and the fauni were heard in lonely woods. Do not try to hear words. Just listen.",times:2,every:"day",prompt:"What did I hear when I stopped? What did I not notice until I had been still for a while?",premade:"hedge-hearing-the-land-walk"}],rite:{title:"Greeting Faunus",summary:"A short Roman-form introduction in a green place: washed hands, covered head, Janus first, then milk and wine poured on the ground for Faunus, and a few minutes of listening.",minutes:20,ingredients:[{name:"A small bottle of water to wash your hands, and a cloth"},{name:"A scarf, hood or fold of cloth to cover your head"},{name:"A little milk in a small flask or cup"},{name:"A little wine or grape juice in a small flask or cup",ref:"traditional/tool/chalice"},{name:"A sprig of oak, pine or any fallen leafy twig",ref:"traditional/herb/oak",optional:!0},{name:"Frankincense and a burner, if you are at home",ref:"traditional/tool/incense_burner",optional:!0}],preparation:"Choose a green place where you can stand undisturbed for twenty minutes: a wood, a field edge, a park, your garden or a balcony full of plants. Tell someone where you are going. Do not light anything outdoors. Read the words through until you can say them without stumbling.",steps:[{title:"Wash and veil",instructions:"Pour a little water over your hands and dry them. Draw your hood or cloth over the back of your head, as a Roman did to pray."},{title:"Janus first",instructions:"Janus, god of doors and beginnings, is called first in Roman prayer. Outdoors, give him a word and a drop of wine on the ground.",spoken:`Father Janus, opener of the way,
be honoured, and carry my words through.`},{title:"Call Faunus",instructions:"Face the trees or the open field. Hold the twig if you have one.",spoken:`Faunus, god of the woods and the pasture,
keeper of flocks, voice in the quiet places,
or by whatever name you rightly wish to be called:
I come to learn who you are.
Be honoured by this milk and this wine.`},{title:"Pour",instructions:"Pour a little milk and then a little wine onto the ground, at the foot of a tree or on grass, not onto a path or into water.",spoken:`Faunus, I ask nothing today but to be known to you,
and to learn to listen.`},{title:"Listen",instructions:"Stand or sit still and listen for a few minutes. Let whatever sounds come, come. Do not hurry to interpret them.",minutes:4,timed:!0},{title:"Vesta last, and turn",instructions:"Name Vesta last, as Roman prayer ended with her. Turn round to your right and uncover your head.",spoken:"Vesta, keeper of the hearth, be honoured, last as always."}],closing:"Leave the place as you found it, or cleaner: pick up any litter on your way out. Take the twig home for your shrine if it was fallen, never broken from a living tree. Write down the date and what you heard: you have begun.",safety:"Tell someone where you are going, keep to safe ground, and keep away from livestock with young. Pour only small amounts; milk and wine in quantity are not good for soil or animals, and grapes and wine are toxic to dogs. If you do this at home with incense, never leave it unattended."}},{kind:"study",title:"Woods, flocks and voices",intro:"Five lessons, about two days each. They go from who Faunus was in Roman legend, to his voice and his oracle, to the country festival and his temple, to how he is honoured today, and finally to the hard parts: the Lupercalia, the stories of chasing nymphs, and the long road from Faunus to the modern Horned God.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"faunus-who",title:"Who Faunus is",summary:"The god of woods and pasture, the old king of Latium, the fauni, and his kin in the Roman countryside.",minutes:6,sections:[{heading:"The god of the green places",body:`Faunus is the god of the places between the farm and the wild: woodland, rough pasture, the edges where flocks graze and wolves might come. He watches over herds and herdsmen, and he is a god of the fertility of animals and land. Horace, who had a farm in the Sabine hills, says Faunus often leaves his Arcadian mountain for Horace\u2019s hill, Lucretilis, and keeps the summer heat and rain off his goats (Odes 1.17).

He is not a god of cities or temples first. He belongs to the countryside, and most of what we know about how he was honoured comes from country life as poets saw it.`},{heading:"The old king",body:`Roman legend made Faunus one of the first kings of Latium. Virgil gives the line: Saturn, then Picus, then Faunus, then Latinus, the king who welcomed Aeneas (Aeneid 7.45\u201349). In this telling Faunus was a wise ruler of the old, simple times who became a god after his death. Some Roman writers said he was the first to set up worship of the gods in Latium.

This is a typical Roman way of thinking about very old gods: as kings and founders of a distant past. It ties Faunus to Saturn and the Golden Age, the time before cities, when people lived from flocks and the woods.`},{heading:"Faunus and the fauni",body:`Romans spoke of Faunus as one god, and also of fauni in the plural: a whole kind of rustic spirits. Lucretius, the Epicurean poet of the first century BC, says country people believed that satyrs, nymphs and fauni haunted lonely places, playing pipes and filling the silence of the night with music, and he explains it all as echoes (On the Nature of Things 4.580\u2013594). The poet\u2019s scepticism tells us what ordinary people believed.

Faunus also had a female counterpart, Fauna, called his wife or sister. Some Roman writers identified her with the Bona Dea, the \u201CGood Goddess\u201D honoured by women in secret rites (Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.12.21\u201327), though how old or certain that identification was is unclear.`},{heading:"His kin and his signs",body:`Faunus shares the countryside with other gods. Silvanus was the god of the woods and of boundaries, very popular with ordinary men, especially farmers and slaves; Cato records an offering for the health of cattle made in the woods to Mars Silvanus, which women were not allowed to see (On Agriculture 83). Pales protected flocks, with her festival, the Parilia, on 21 April. Faunus and Silvanus were sometimes blended, and many inscriptions to Silvanus could equally suit Faunus.

His signs are the goat and the goatskin, horns, the shepherd\u2019s crook and the reed pipes, which came to him with Pan. The wolf is his neighbour, the thing he keeps from the flock. Oak and pine, the trees of the Italian hills, suit him.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Virgil (Aeneid 7.45\u201349) gives the royal line; Horace (Odes 1.17 and 3.18) shows him in country worship; Lucretius (4.580\u2013594) describes belief in fauni. Cato (On Agriculture 83) gives the rite for Mars Silvanus. Macrobius (1.12) reports the identification of Fauna with the Bona Dea.",unknown:"The real origin of Faunus, and whether the king of legend preserves any older memory, is unknown. The relations between Faunus, Silvanus, Inuus and Pan were blurred in antiquity and are debated now."},inPractice:"Find one green place near you that you can visit often this month, however small, and make it \u201Chis\u201D for the journey. Notice who else uses it: birds, dogs, insects, people.",reflect:["Where are the edges between the tame and the wild in your own life?","How does it feel to meet a god who belongs to the countryside rather than the city?"],further:["Horace, Odes 1.17 and 3.18 (public-domain translations on Project Gutenberg)","Peter F. Dorcey, The Cult of Silvanus: A Study in Roman Folk Religion (1992)"],library:["traditional/deity/faunus","traditional/deity/saturn","traditional/herb/oak","traditional/herb/pine"]},{id:"faunus-voice",title:"The voice in the wood",summary:"Why the Romans said Faunus speaks, his oracle at Albunea, and Numa sleeping on a fleece.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The god who speaks",body:`Varro, the great Roman antiquarian, says the fauni were gods of the Latins who were believed to speak in wild places, in the old verse called Saturnian, and that their name came from fari, \u201Cto speak\u201D (On the Latin Language 7.36). Other Romans explained the name from favere, \u201Cto be favourable\u201D. Either way, the speaking god stuck. Roman historians tell of voices heard from woods at moments of crisis, sometimes credited to Faunus.

A god who speaks is a god you can consult. Faunus was one of the few Roman gods with an oracle, though it was a country oracle in a grove, not a great temple like Apollo\u2019s at Delphi.`},{heading:"The oracle at Albunea",body:`In the seventh book of the Aeneid, King Latinus is troubled by omens about his daughter\u2019s marriage. He goes to the oracle of his father Faunus, in the grove below Albunea, a sulphurous spring in a dark wood. There, Virgil says, the priestess brings gifts, and lies down at night on the spread fleeces of slaughtered sheep, and sleeps, and sees many visions and hears voices, and speaks with the gods. Latinus does this himself and hears a voice from the grove telling him to marry his daughter to a stranger (7.81\u2013106).

This is incubation: sleeping in a holy place to receive a dream. It was practised at many ancient sanctuaries, most famously those of the healing god Asclepius.`},{heading:"Numa and the fleece",body:`Ovid tells a similar story in the Fasti. In a time of failed harvests, King Numa goes to an ancient grove of Faunus, sacrifices two sheep, one to Faunus and one to Sleep, spreads their fleeces on the ground, and sleeps on them, having fasted and kept away from his wife. Faunus appears and gives him a riddling instruction, which Numa\u2019s wife Egeria helps him understand (Fasti 4.649\u2013672).

These stories belong to their setting: a sacred grove, a sacrifice, a king. They are learned about rather than copied. But they show how Romans pictured the god: as someone who answers the patient, in the quiet of the night, in a voice that needs some thought to understand.`},{heading:"Listening today",body:`Modern devotees do not sleep on fleeces in sacred groves. What they do is simpler: they go to quiet green places and listen, and they pay attention to dreams. Some keep a dream journal while working with Faunus. Some ask him a question at the end of the day, go to sleep, and note what they remember in the morning, without forcing an answer.

The honest way to do this is lightly. A rustle in the leaves is a rustle in the leaves. What matters is the practice of stopping, being quiet in a living place, and listening more than you speak.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Varro (On the Latin Language 7.36) gives the speaking fauni. Virgil (Aeneid 7.81\u2013106) describes the oracle at Albunea, and Ovid (Fasti 4.649\u2013672) Numa\u2019s incubation. Incubation is well attested at other ancient sanctuaries.",unknown:"Where Albunea was (Tibur or near Lavinium) is debated, and whether a real oracle of Faunus operated there in historical times is uncertain; Virgil and Ovid may be drawing on antiquarian lore rather than living practice."},inPractice:"Keep a small notebook by your bed for the rest of the journey. On three nights, before sleep, ask Faunus a simple question about your life outdoors or your care for animals, and write down whatever you remember on waking. Do not read too much into it.",reflect:["When did you last spend time in a quiet place without speaking or looking at a screen?","What is the difference between listening and waiting for an answer?"],further:["Virgil, Aeneid 7.81\u2013106 (public-domain translations on Project Gutenberg)","Ovid, Fasti 4.649\u2013672 (Frazer\u2019s Loeb translation on LacusCurtius)"],library:["traditional/deity/faunus","traditional/herb/mugwort"],premade:["hedge-hearing-the-land-walk"]},{id:"faunus-feasts",title:"The Nones of December and the Ides of February",summary:"Horace\u2019s village Faunalia, the temple on Tiber Island built from graziers\u2019 fines, and what country people gave him.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Horace\u2019s Faunalia",body:`The best picture of Faunus being worshipped is a short poem by Horace, Odes 3.18. He addresses the god as the lover of fleeing nymphs, and asks him to pass gently over his boundaries and sunny fields and to go away kind to the young of the flock. In return, a kid will fall at the end of the year, there will be plenty of wine in the mixing bowl, and the old altar will smoke with incense.

Then he paints the day. When the Nones of December come round, the whole herd plays in the grassy meadow; the village keeps holiday in the fields with the oxen at rest; the wolf wanders among lambs that are not afraid; the wood scatters its leaves for him; and the ditch-digger is glad to stamp the hated earth in a three-step dance. It is a festival of rest, safety and joy at the year\u2019s end.`},{heading:"The temple on the island",body:`Faunus also had a public temple in Rome, on Tiber Island. Livy tells that in 196 BC two plebeian aediles prosecuted graziers who had been pasturing their animals on public land, and used the fines to build a temple of Faunus; it was dedicated two years later (33.42.10; 34.53.4). Its anniversary fell on the Ides of February, 13 February, and Ovid mentions it there (Fasti 2.193\u2013194).

There is a neat fit in it: a god of pasture, honoured with money taken from people who had abused the common pasture.`},{heading:"What he was given",body:`Country offerings to Faunus, as Horace and other poets describe them, were wine, milk, incense and, in the old way, a kid or lamb. Ovid has Numa sacrifice a sheep. Tibullus and the pastoral poets picture shepherds pouring milk to the gods of the flock. Music and dancing were part of his festival, and the reed pipes were his instrument.

Modern devotees keep the bloodless parts: milk, wine, honey, bread, incense, music and dance, poured and played outdoors. Care for animals is a natural modern offering: support for a farm sanctuary, a donation of food to an animal shelter, or simply looking after the animals in your own home well.`},{heading:"A modern Faunalia",body:`Roman polytheists today keep the Faunalia on 5 December, the Nones, often in the spirit of Horace\u2019s poem: a gathering outdoors if the weather allows, or a warm meal indoors with a window open to the cold; an offering of wine, milk and incense; music; and a dance, however clumsy. Many also honour him on 13 February, his temple\u2019s day.

A simple home Faunalia: on 5 December, go for a walk in a wood or park and pour a little milk and wine at the foot of a tree; at home, light incense on the shrine, play or sing something, and give to an animal charity. Rest your own \u201Coxen\u201D: take the day as lightly as you can.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Horace, Odes 3.18, describes the Faunalia on the Nones of December. Livy (33.42.10 and 34.53.4) and Ovid (Fasti 2.193\u2013194) give the temple on Tiber Island and its date.",unknown:"Whether the Nones of December was a widely kept rural festival or Horace\u2019s local custom is uncertain; it is not marked as a public festival in the calendars. The rites at the Tiber Island temple are not described."},inPractice:"Mark 5 December and 13 February in your calendar. Choose an animal charity, farm sanctuary or wildlife group you would like to support in his name.",reflect:["Horace\u2019s Faunalia is a day when even the wolf is gentle. What would a day of real safety and rest look like for you?","Which animals depend on you, and how well are you keeping them?"],further:["Horace, Odes 3.18 (Latin at The Latin Library; public-domain translations on Project Gutenberg)","H. H. Scullard, Festivals and Ceremonies of the Roman Republic (1981)"],library:["traditional/deity/faunus","traditional/herb/frankincense"],premade:["green-tree-befriending-offering"]},{id:"faunus-today",title:"Honouring Faunus today",summary:"Roman polytheists and modern Pagans, the household Faunus, and how to set up his shrine.",minutes:6,sections:[{heading:"Who honours him now",body:`Faunus is honoured today by Roman polytheists, people practising the Religio Romana or Cultus Deorum, a small reconstructed religion with groups in Italy, organisations such as Nova Roma, and many solitary practitioners. They keep the Faunalia and his February days, and honour him as a guardian of flocks, fields and wild places.

Many modern Pagans and witches also honour him, often blended with Pan and with the Horned God of Wicca, as a god of nature, the wild and the body. That is a modern way of seeing him, shaped by nineteenth-century poetry and twentieth-century religion (lesson five). It can be done with real care. It helps to know when you are speaking to the Roman god of the pastures and when to the modern god of the wild.`},{heading:"Faunus through the year",body:`A household Faunus has a few natural moments: the Ides of February for his temple, the Nones of December for his country feast, and spring, when lambs and kids are born. Most of all, he is honoured whenever you go out into green places with attention and come back having left them better.

Care for animals belongs to him: the pets in your home, farm animals, and wild creatures. So does care for woods and pastures: planting hedges and trees, joining a conservation day, choosing food from farms that treat animals well.`},{heading:"Setting up his shrine",body:`Put Faunus\u2019s shrine by a window that looks out on something green, or outside if you have a garden or balcony. You need a small cup for milk or wine, an offering dish, an incense burner, and a candle for indoor rites. For his image, use a small statue of a faun or of Pan, a goat or wolf figure, a set of reed pipes, or simply a fallen branch of oak or pine. Green and brown are modern colours for him; moss agate and green aventurine are modern stones.

Keep the shrine natural and a little untidy, as befits a god of the woods: a feather, a pine cone, a stone from a walk, changed with the seasons. Never take anything living from a wild place for it.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Modern Roman polytheist groups publish their calendars, including the Faunalia. The modern Pagan Pan and Horned God are well documented as modern developments (Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon).",unknown:"There is little ancient evidence for household cult of Faunus in towns; he was a country god. The everyday practices here are modern, built on the poets\u2019 picture of his worship."},inPractice:"Set up his shrine by a green window this week, with something you found on a walk. Make your first gift to the animal charity or sanctuary you chose.",reflect:["Do you find yourself drawn to the Roman Faunus, the Greek Pan, the modern Horned God, or a blend? Can you say why?","What green place could you help look after this year?"],further:["Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (1999; 2nd edn 2019), on Pan and the Horned God","Nova Roma\u2019s published calendar and religious guides (novaroma.org)"],library:["traditional/tool/altar","traditional/crystal/moss_agate","traditional/crystal/green_aventurine","traditional/candle/green","traditional/candle/brown"],premade:["folk-lararium-offering","green-land-spirit-garden-offering"]},{id:"faunus-hard",title:"The Lupercalia, the nymphs and the Horned God",summary:"What the Lupercalia was and why it is not revived, the stories of pursuit, and how Faunus became Pan and Pan became the Horned God.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The Lupercalia",body:`The Lupercalia, on 15 February, was one of the oldest and strangest Roman festivals. At the Lupercal, a cave at the foot of the Palatine where the wolf was said to have nursed Romulus and Remus, the priests called Luperci sacrificed goats and a dog. Two young men were touched on the forehead with the bloody knife, the blood was wiped off with wool soaked in milk, and they had to laugh. Then the Luperci, wearing only goatskins, ran around the Palatine striking people they met with strips of goat hide, and women held out their hands to be struck, believing it helped them conceive (Plutarch, Romulus 21).

Which god it honoured was already argued in antiquity. Livy says Pan of Arcadia (1.5); Ovid gives it to Faunus (Fasti 2.267\u2013452). It lasted until the end of the fifth century AD, when Pope Gelasius wrote against it. In 44 BC, at the Lupercalia, Mark Antony, running as a Lupercus, offered Julius Caesar a crown, which he refused.

Nothing of this should be recreated: not the sacrifice, not the striking. Modern Roman polytheists who mark the day do it with offerings of milk and wine, with purification of the house, and with prayers for fertility and health.`},{heading:"The lover of fleeing nymphs",body:`Horace calls Faunus \u201Clover of the fleeing nymphs\u201D, and the fauni and satyrs of art and poetry chase nymphs who run from them. Ovid tells a comic story in the Fasti: Faunus creeps at night into the cave where Hercules and the Lydian queen Omphale are sleeping, having swapped clothes, and tries to assault the one in women\u2019s clothes, only to find Hercules, who throws him across the cave. That, Ovid says, is why Faunus hates clothes and his runners go naked (2.303\u2013358).

The story is told as a joke, and it is a joke about an attempted rape. It is honest to see that. Many modern devotees honour Faunus\u2019s wildness and his delight in the body while setting this side of the old stories firmly aside. Desire in his name is free and mutual, or it is not his.`},{heading:"From Faunus to the Horned God",body:`Romans matched Faunus with Pan, the Arcadian goat-god. In the nineteenth century, English poets and writers revived Pan as a symbol of wild nature against the industrial city; Kenneth Grahame\u2019s The Wind in the Willows (1908) has a chapter in which two animals meet him at dawn. In the twentieth century, the writer Margaret Murray argued, wrongly, that witches had secretly worshipped a horned god since prehistory, and Gerald Gardner\u2019s Wicca took up a Horned God who drew on Pan, the Celtic Cernunnos and others.

So when a modern book calls Faunus the Horned God, it is joining three things: an old Roman god of pastures, a Greek god of Arcadia, and a modern god of the Wiccan circle. All three can be honoured. They are not the same, and the history is clear about which is which.`},{heading:"What not to do",body:"Do not recreate the Lupercalia or strike anyone. Do not take living plants, eggs or animals from wild places for your shrine. Do not leave food that can harm animals; chocolate, grapes, raisins, onions and garlic are toxic to dogs and many others. Do not go into woods or fields at night alone. And do not use the wild god as an excuse for carelessness: Faunus guarded the flock against the wolf, and his worshippers were the people who did the work of keeping animals alive."}],knowAndDont:{known:"Plutarch (Romulus 21), Ovid (Fasti 2.267\u2013452) and Livy (1.5) describe the Lupercalia; Gelasius\u2019 letter against it survives; Antony\u2019s offer of the crown is in Plutarch and Suetonius. Horace (Odes 3.18) and Ovid (Fasti 2.303\u2013358) give the nymphs and the Omphale story. The modern history of Pan and the Horned God is set out in Hutton\u2019s The Triumph of the Moon.",unknown:"The original meaning of the Lupercalia, and its god, are debated; ancient explanations disagree. How far the Roman Faunus was ever seen as a single great god of nature, as modern Pagans see him, is doubtful."},inPractice:"If you keep 15 February, do it with milk, wine and a cleaning of the house, not with any part of the ancient rite. Decide which of the three, the Roman, the Greek and the modern god, you mean when you call him.",reflect:["How do you honour wildness without honouring harm?","Does it matter to you that the Horned God is modern? Why, or why not?"],further:["Plutarch, Life of Romulus 21 (public-domain translation by Bernadotte Perrin on LacusCurtius)","Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (1999; 2nd edn 2019)"],library:["traditional/deity/faunus","traditional/deity/cernunnos","traditional/deity/mars"]}],practices:[{id:"faunus-study-green",title:"A green place, every day",instructions:"Through the study stage, spend at least five minutes a day in a green place, or looking out at one if you cannot go out. Note one living thing you saw or heard.",times:10,every:"day",prompt:"What living thing did I meet today? What was it doing?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Nine days of listening and keeping",intro:"Nine days of a short outdoor practice: listen, pour a little, and do one thing for an animal or a wild place. Nine-day periods were used in Roman religion for other rites; here nine frames a practice, not a claim about ancient custom.",days:9,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"faunus-nine-days",title:"Listen, pour, keep",instructions:`Each day, go to your green place, or open a window onto one. Cover your head and give a word to Janus. Then say: \u201CFaunus, keeper of the flock, be honoured. I listen.\u201D Stand still and listen for five minutes. Pour a few drops of milk or wine at the foot of a tree or on grass (indoors, into his dish). Then do one thing for an animal or a wild place: refill a bird bath, pick up litter, walk a neighbour\u2019s dog, give to an animal charity, check on your own animals with real attention. Name Vesta last, turn to your right, and uncover.

If you could not get outside, listen at an open window and say so honestly.`,times:9,every:"day",prompt:"What did I hear today? What did I do for an animal or a wild place?"},{id:"faunus-music",title:"Music for the god",instructions:"Once in the nine days, make music for Faunus: play an instrument, whistle, sing, or tap out a rhythm on a log, outdoors if you can. It does not need to be good. Horace\u2019s ditch-digger danced on the hard earth in clumsy triple time. If you like, dance too.",times:1,prompt:"How did it feel to make music for a god, with no audience but him?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"At the edge of the pasture",intro:"A few days to stop and decide. Faunus asks for attention to wild places and care for animals more than formal ritual, and a dedication to him is a promise to keep going outside, keep listening, and keep looking after what is in your care. Stopping, pausing, or choosing another god or path is a good outcome.",days:3,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"faunus-discern-walk",title:"A long walk",instructions:"On one of the three days, take a longer walk than usual through the greenest country you can reach safely. Carry the questions below with you, and stop to write down answers when they come.",times:1,prompt:"What did the walk settle, and what is still unsettled?",premade:"hedge-hearing-the-land-walk"}],questions:["Did the nine days feel like devotion, or like an outdoor chore with a prayer attached? Either answer is useful.","Can I keep going out to green places, and keep a small shrine and his two feasts, for a year?","Am I comfortable with the Roman manner of prayer, or do I want a looser, wilder way with him? Do I know which I am choosing?","How do I feel about the Lupercalia and the old stories of pursuit?","Am I drawn to Faunus himself, or to Pan, the Horned God, Silvanus or Diana, who might fit better?","Would I rather keep walking and listening without dedicating? That is a perfectly good choice."]},{kind:"dedication",title:"The dedication",intro:"If you have decided to go ahead, hold this outdoors in a green place you love, ideally on 5 December, the Nones, or on 13 February, his temple\u2019s day, or on a day in spring when lambs are in the fields. It is a self-dedication, not an initiation, and it does not make you a Lupercus or a priest. You are dedicating yourself as a devotee who listens in wild places and keeps what is in your care.",days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Self-dedication to Faunus",summary:"Hands washed and head covered, Janus first, a prayer to Faunus in a green place, a spoken dedication, milk, wine, honey and bread poured and laid at a tree, a tune, and Vesta last.",minutes:40,ingredients:[{name:"A bottle of water and a cloth for washing hands"},{name:"A hood, scarf or fold of cloth for your head"},{name:"A small flask of milk"},{name:"A small flask of wine or grape juice",ref:"traditional/tool/chalice"},{name:"A piece of bread and a little honey"},{name:"A fallen sprig of oak or pine",ref:"traditional/herb/oak"},{name:"Your written dedication"},{name:"Something to make music with: pipes, a whistle, your voice",optional:!0},{name:"Frankincense and a burner for the shrine at home afterwards",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense",optional:!0}],preparation:"Choose your green place and check the weather and the way. Tell someone where you are going. Write your dedication: who you are, that you dedicate yourself to Faunus as a devotee, for how long (a year is wise), and what you will do: keep his feasts, go out and listen, care for animals and one wild place, and give to an animal or conservation cause. Read it aloud until it flows. Pack everything in a small bag; carry your litter home.",steps:[{title:"Wash and veil",instructions:"At your place, pour water over your hands and dry them. Cover your head."},{title:"Janus first",instructions:"Pour a drop of wine on the ground for Janus.",spoken:`Father Janus, first in every prayer,
open the way to Faunus.`},{title:"The invocation",instructions:"Face the trees or the open land. Raise your hands, palms up, holding the sprig.",spoken:`Faunus, god of the woods and the pasture,
keeper of flocks and herds and those who tend them,
voice in the quiet places, son of Picus, grandson of Saturn,
or by whatever name it is right to call you:
I pray good prayers,
that you may be willing and favourable
to me, my house and my household,
and to every creature in my care.`},{title:"Read the dedication",instructions:"Read your dedication aloud, clearly and in full. If you stumble, stop and begin again from the start.",minutes:5},{title:"Offer",instructions:"Pour a little milk and a little wine at the foot of a tree. Lay a small piece of bread with a drop of honey on it beside them, and the sprig.",spoken:`Faunus, be honoured by this milk, this wine, this bread and honey.
As I have spoken, so I will do.`},{title:"A tune",instructions:"Play, whistle or sing something short. If you feel like it, dance a few steps.",minutes:2},{title:"Listen",instructions:"Stand or sit still and listen.",minutes:5,timed:!0},{title:"Vesta last, and turn",instructions:"Name Vesta, turn to your right, and uncover your head.",spoken:"Vesta, keeper of the hearth, be honoured. It is done."}],closing:"Leave the bread small enough for birds to take, or take it home for the compost if animals or livestock could be harmed. Carry all your litter home. Eat the rest of the bread and honey yourself, ideally with someone. At home, light incense on his shrine and write down what you heard. Note the date for your rededication, or keep it at each Faunalia.",safety:"Tell someone where you are going. Stay on safe ground and away from livestock with young, and from water and cliff edges. Do not light any flame outdoors. Pour only small amounts; never leave wine, grapes or chocolate where dogs or wildlife can reach them. Honey should not be given to babies under a year old."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"Keeping the flock",intro:"Faunus\u2019s ongoing worship is seasonal and outdoor: time in green places, care for animals and wild land, his two feasts, and a small shrine that changes with the year.",days:4,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"faunus-ongoing-walk",title:"A monthly walk",instructions:"Once a month, walk in a green place with his prayer in the Roman form, listen for a while, pour a little milk or wine at a tree, and pick up litter on the way out. Change something on his shrine to match the season.",times:12,every:"month",prompt:"What had changed in the green place since last month?",premade:"green-tree-befriending-offering"},{id:"faunus-ongoing-feasts",title:"His feasts",instructions:"On 13 February, the Ides, an offering of milk, wine and incense for his temple\u2019s day; on 15 February, if you keep it, a cleaning of the house and prayers for health. On 5 December, the Faunalia: a walk, an offering at a tree, music, a gift to an animal charity, and as much rest as you can manage. Rededicate at the Faunalia, reading your dedication and saying honestly how you kept it.",times:2,every:"holy-day",prompt:"What did the year\u2019s animals and green places give me, and what did I give back?",premade:"folk-lararium-offering"}]}],sources:["Horace, Odes 1.17 and 3.18 (Latin, The Latin Library: https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/horace/carm3.shtml); translations and paraphrases original","Virgil, Aeneid 7.45\u201349 and 7.81\u2013106 (Latin, The Latin Library: https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/vergil/aen7.shtml)","Ovid, Fasti 2.193\u2013194, 2.267\u2013452 and 4.649\u2013672 (Latin, The Latin Library: https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti2.shtml)","Varro, On the Latin Language 7.36, the speaking fauni (paraphrased)","Livy, History of Rome 1.5, 33.42.10 and 34.53.4 (paraphrased)","Lucretius, On the Nature of Things 4.580\u2013594 (paraphrased)","Plutarch, Life of Romulus 21, the Lupercalia (paraphrased)","Cato, On Agriculture 83, the offering to Mars Silvanus; Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.12.21\u201327, Fauna and the Bona Dea (paraphrased)","Gelasius I, Letter against Andromachus, on the Lupercalia (paraphrased)","H. H. Scullard, Festivals and Ceremonies of the Roman Republic (London, 1981)","Peter F. Dorcey, The Cult of Silvanus (Leiden, 1992)","Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (Oxford, 1999; 2nd edn 2019)"]};return c(m);})();
